# The purchased theme is the served UI's design system, so the handful of files
# the application actually loads MUST reach the image. Everything else under
# assets/ is demo material and stays out.
#
# This was the Linux deployment bug: `assets` alone excluded the whole tree, so
# /app/assets did not exist in the image, collectstatic found only the app's own
# static dirs, and the browser got 404 for every theme bundle.
#
# Docker evaluates every pattern per path and the last match wins, so these
# re-inclusions must stay after the directory exclusion above.
assets
!assets/css/style.bundle.rtl.css
!assets/js/scripts.bundle.js
!assets/plugins/global/plugins.bundle.rtl.css
# ApexCharts, which every chart in the panel is drawn with since 1.3.12. It
# ships only inside this bundle — the theme has no standalone build — so all
# 3.6 MB of it is runtime, not demo material.
!assets/plugins/global/plugins.bundle.js
!assets/plugins/global/fonts/keenicons
!assets/fonts
# The lead follow-up calendar (1.7.1) draws with this bundle — the theme has
# no standalone build of it either. Missing until 1.7.5: the blanket `assets`
# exclusion above was never given a matching re-inclusion for it, so the
# calendar page loaded, called FullCalendar, and there was no FullCalendar —
# in any deployment that actually builds this Dockerfile, never in `runserver`,
# which serves straight from the repository and never consults this file.
!assets/plugins/custom/fullcalendar
# The leads Kanban board (lead_kanban feature, 2026-09-05) draws with this one
# — same reasoning as fullcalendar directly above, and the same mistake would
# repeat here if this line were forgotten: the theme ships no standalone
# jKanban build either.
!assets/plugins/custom/jkanban
src
asides
dashboards
layouts
pages
toolbars
widgets
utilities
account
apps
authentication
index.html
landing.html
code_dumper*.py

# --- P0R.4 build-context hardening ---
# The runtime image only needs manage.py, config/, and the first-party app
# packages (models/services/selectors/views/urls/templates/static). Everything
# below is developer-, ops-, or documentation-only material that must never be
# shipped inside the application image. Ops scripts and Nginx config are
# bind-mounted from the host by compose.yml, so excluding them here does not
# affect any running service. Verified against compose.yml service commands.
# NOTE: this reduces shipped file count only. It does NOT hide readable Python
# business logic — backend packaging/compilation remains a separate P12 gate.
docs
*.md
**/tests
scripts
nginx
compose*.yml
requirements-direct.txt
# The bare `__pycache__`/`*.pyc`/`*.log` entries near the top of this file are
# root-anchored only: a .dockerignore `*` never crosses `/`, so they do not
# exclude nested directories. These recursive forms are what actually keep
# developer bytecode and logs out of the image.
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/*.log
# The build recipe is read by the builder itself and is not needed at runtime.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.editorconfig
.vscode
.idea
*.swp
.DS_Store
**/*.map
